WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.890
"The Batman" is barely out in theaters worldwide,
and people are already fan-casting their dream

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:06.890
follow-up.

00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:11.560
A sequel to "The Batman" hasn't officially
been announced as of the making of this video.

00:00:11.560 --> 00:00:15.240
That said, the film teases more adventures
from the Dark Knight and his rogues' gallery

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.000
of foes moving forward.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.980
Furthermore, producer Dylan Clark has a timeframe
in mind for the seemingly inevitable follow-up

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:26.859
to Matt Reeves' gritty blockbuster, and Warner
Bros. intends to give one of the movie's villains

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.939
his very own spin-off series.

00:00:28.939 --> 00:00:33.079
All in all, it seems that the studio heads
and filmmakers have high hopes for this iteration

00:00:33.079 --> 00:00:34.730
of the "Batman" franchise.

00:00:34.730 --> 00:00:38.620
Of course, even the slightest possibility
of a sequel to "The Batman" is enough of a

00:00:38.620 --> 00:00:43.280
reason to get fans talking about the characters
they'd like to see make an appearance down

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.280
the line.

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:49.100
Penguin, the Riddler, and the Joker have all
had time in the spotlight in recent years.

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.720
But the "Batman" vault boasts a wide array
of memorable baddies, many of whom have arguably

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.629
been overlooked, underserved, or flat-out
ignored in the live-action movies.

00:00:58.629 --> 00:01:02.749
Gotham's Caped Crusader has a long history
with Mr. Freeze that's barely been explored

00:01:02.749 --> 00:01:07.439
in the films, and many fans would no doubt
love to see a serious exploration of that

00:01:07.439 --> 00:01:11.890
conflict now that they've had time to recover
from Arnold Schwarzenegger's ill-fated turn

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:15.140
as the character in 1997's "Batman &amp; Robin."

00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:17.369
"What killed the dinosaurs?

00:01:17.369 --> 00:01:19.600
The ice age!"

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.210
It remains to be seen if "The Batman" filmmakers
include Mr. Freeze in a future project.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.890
Numerous "Batman" fans on social media have
been calling for Mr. Freeze to appear in the

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:34.080
sequel to Matt Reeves' film, causing the character
to trend online in the process.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:38.890
Meanwhile, over on Instagram, artist Mizuri
has shown how the iconic villain could look

00:01:38.890 --> 00:01:42.180
if he were to be portrayed by English actor
Mark Strong.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:47.000
The chilling illustration depicts Strong with
icy white skin, sporting a pair of glasses

00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:49.369
that accentuate Freeze's red eyes.

00:01:49.369 --> 00:01:54.310
The character's trademark black suit is also
on display, giving him an imposing and muscular

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:55.310
appearance.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:59.560
The image is capped off with blue hues which
really enhance the freezing factor that fans

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:02.470
expect to be associated with this particular
villain.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:07.219
The picture is much scarier than Arnold Schwarzenegger's
version of the character from "Batman &amp; Robin,"

00:02:07.219 --> 00:02:11.950
and some of Mizuri's followers support the
idea of seeing Strong cast as a formidable

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:12.950
foe.

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:13.950
One commenter wrote,

00:02:13.950 --> 00:02:15.110
"Mark as Freeze would be awesome.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.970
I always thought he suited the role."

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:21.730
Elsewhere, some followers revealed how they'd
like to see Mr. Freeze used in the sequel.

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.730
One user wrote,

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.860
"I really love how they portrayed his character
in the Arkham games and I really want to see

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.890
that version in a live-action film."

00:02:28.890 --> 00:02:33.230
Mark Strong is no stranger to comic book movie
roles, having previously starred in Matthew

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:38.000
Vaughn's "Kingsman: The Secret Service" and
the sequel "Kingsman: The Golden Circle,"

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.480
as well as "Kick-Ass."

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.540
He's also no stranger to the DC Universe,
having appeared in "Green Lantern" and "Shazam!."

00:02:44.540 --> 00:02:48.530
Back when the movie was in pre-production,
he was also heavily rumored to be in the running

00:02:48.530 --> 00:02:53.959
for the role of Lex Luthor in "Batman v Superman:
Dawn of Justice," so it's understandable why

00:02:53.959 --> 00:02:58.950
superhero fans continue to dream cast the
Englishman as their favorite characters.

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:02.800
But what are the possibilities of Strong gracing
the sequel to "The Batman?"

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:06.040
There is no record of Strong stating his interest
in the role.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:09.730
Furthermore, he appears to be taking a break
from DC flicks for now.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:12.790
The actor previously revealed that he will
not be in "Shazam!

00:03:12.790 --> 00:03:15.610
Fury of the Gods," but could he be deceiving
us?

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:19.900
Dr. Thaddeus Sivana's burgeoning alliance
with Mister Mind following the events of the

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:24.470
first film suggests that he has unfinished
business in this franchise after all.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.459
"What in God's name?"

00:03:26.459 --> 00:03:31.923
"I made the gods, Doctor – not the other
way around."

00:03:31.923 --> 00:03:35.850
The filmmakers might opt to catch up with
Strong's evil scientist and his quest to take

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:37.860
over the Seven Realms down the line.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.340
That would give him a window to play Freeze,
right?

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:44.799
Additionally, "The Batman" isn't part of the
same cinematic universe as "Shazam!."

00:03:44.799 --> 00:03:48.570
Theoretically, that would mean that Strong
could play the Gotham City menace without

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:51.459
having to worry about crossing paths with
his "Shazam!"

00:03:51.459 --> 00:03:52.459
antagonist.

00:03:52.459 --> 00:03:54.510
It's certainly an idea worth considering.

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:57.239
"The Iceman cometh!"

